# Draw Me A STIX - example configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads .env automatically from the project root. Everything
# here is OPTIONAL: `docker compose up` works with no .env at all, because the
# app itself needs no configuration - it is a static site and all of an
# analyst's data lives in their own browser.
#
# These variables only matter if you also run the enrichment sidecar:
#
#   docker compose --profile enrich up

# --- Enrichment sidecar ----------------------------------------------------

# Bearer token the sidecar requires on every request. Leave it empty and the
# sidecar generates a random one at startup and prints it in its logs - fine
# for a quick local try, useless for anything that has to survive a restart.
#
# Generate one:  openssl rand -base64 32
ENRICHER_TOKEN=

# Browser origins allowed to call the sidecar (comma-separated). CORS is
# strict: a browser will refuse the response for any origin not listed here.
# Set this to wherever you actually serve the app.
ENRICHER_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8000

# --- Beyond these two ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The sidecar reads a dozen more variables - cache TTLs, tool and HTTP
# timeouts, result caps. Every one of them is documented, with its rationale,
# in enricher/app/config.py. They are not listed here because Compose does not
# forward them: to use one, add it to the `environment:` block of the
# `enricher` service in docker-compose.yml.
#
# The two worth knowing about concern slow public sources, crt.sh above all -
# it alternates between answering in ~40 seconds, instant 502s, and total
# silence, and that is its normal behaviour:
#
#   STIXIT_ENRICHER_HTTP_TIMEOUT (45)  ceiling for ONE attempt
#   STIXIT_ENRICHER_HTTP_BUDGET  (90)  total wait, retries included
